1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,040
If you've spent any time on TikTok or Instagram, you've probably seen "Glucose Goddess" hacks — eating a meal in a specific order to keep blood sugar steadier.

2
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:17,200
It sounds like just another wellness trend, but there's real clinical research behind it.

3
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:25,520
Here's the science: when vegetables and protein hit your stomach before carbs do, digestion slows down.

4
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:40,240
Fiber creates a kind of physical barrier in your gut that delays how fast sugar gets absorbed into your bloodstream, and it also boosts GLP-1, a hormone that makes your insulin response more effective.

5
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:41,280
Dr.

6
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:49,280
Alpana Shukla and her team at Weill Cornell Medicine put this to the test in people with type 2 diabetes.

7
00:00:49,600 --> 00:01:02,720
Same exact meal, different order — vegetables and protein first, carbs last — and post-meal blood sugar spikes dropped by up to 40% compared to eating carbs first.

8
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:08,800
The order that made the difference: vegetables, then protein, then carbs.

9
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:18,160
Start with something like a side salad or steamed vegetables — think of it as the opposite of diving straight into the bread basket at a restaurant.

10
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:24,560
Fiber is the first thing to hit your gut, and it slows down everything that comes after.

11
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:31,440
Next, move to protein — grilled chicken, salmon, eggs, whatever's on your plate.

12
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:40,640
It digests slowly too, which blunts the glucose rise even more, and it keeps you full longer than starting with carbs would.

13
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:45,120
Save the bread, rice, pasta, or fries for last.

14
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:52,080
Same plate, same portions — just reordering it is enough to noticeably flatten the spike.

15
00:01:52,400 --> 00:02:05,520
Jessie Inchauspé, the creator behind "Glucose Goddess," has one more trick that pairs well with this: a tablespoon of vinegar diluted in a glass of water before a carb-heavy meal.

16
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:15,600
Early research suggests the acetic acid in vinegar slows down how quickly your stomach empties, which blunts the spike even further.

17
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:19,360
This isn't a replacement for diabetes medication, though.

18
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:26,480
If you're managing diabetes or prediabetes, check with your doctor before changing how you eat.

19
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:35,280
Otherwise, it's an easy thing to try at your very next meal — vegetables first, protein second, carbs last.
